{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>מִכֹּל שֶׁרֶץ הַמַּיִם</b> — Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Leviticus 11:10) said that \"שֶׁרֶץ\" refers to the small creatures that are generated from the water, and \"נֶפֶשׁ הַחַיָּה\" means male and female. In my opinion, שֶׁרֶץ הַמַּיִם is a general term for the fish that swim in the water, for every expression of שְׁרִיצָה denotes movement. \"וּמִכֹּל נֶפֶשׁ הַחַיָּה\" — these are the creatures of the sea that have legs and walk upon them like the beasts of the field. And behold, they all have one ruling. And the midrash of the Sifra (parashah 3, section 7): \"חַיָּה\" — this is the sea-creature; \"נֶפֶשׁ\" — to include the סִּירָנִי (siren?). Might one think it should defile by tent (i.e., through being under the same roof) like the words of Ben Chakhinai? Scripture says: \"שֶׁקֶץ\" (abomination)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The identification of בֶּן הַסִּירָנִי as a siren is uncertain; the Talmudic term refers to a legendary or composite creature and is rendered here with a question mark to flag the ambiguity."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:52:41.759429939Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}